{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Missing Postgres Service Container in Docker Executor",
    "Explanation": [
      "The docker executor declares only one image, `circleci/node`, so no PostgreSQL service container is attached to the job. Because CircleCI's secondary service containers share the network namespace with the primary container (reachable on localhost), a correctly configured Postgres service would be listening on localhost:5432. The log shows 'connection refused' on every attempt over the full minute, i.e. nothing was ever listening on that port — not a slow start (which would show refusals then a success) and not a DNS/host problem. dockerize itself installed fine (step SUCCESS) and executed normally, so it is not missing. The port 5432 matches Postgres's default, so the port is not wrong. Increasing the timeout or changing the host would not help since no database process exists anywhere in the environment, and removing the wait step would just hide the missing dependency. The pipeline definition is simply missing a Postgres service image in the `docker:` list, so the fix is to add one (e.g. `- image: circleci/postgres:9.6-alpine` with the appropriate POSTGRES_USER/DB env vars)."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"fix\": \"add_postgres_service_image\",",
      "  \"root_cause\": \"no_postgres_service_container\"",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"fix\": \"add_postgres_service_image\",",
        "  \"root_cause\": \"no_postgres_service_container\"",
        "}"
